Notepad Studio — Change Log
===========================

This file opens automatically the first time you launch Notepad Studio after
a new install or an update. To dismiss, just close the tab.

Notepad Studio is a lightweight cross-platform text editor inspired by
Notepad++, written in Rust on GPUI. Apache-2.0.


v0.0.5 — current

  Recent additions:

    1. NPP-style tab right-click context menu — Close, Close Multiple Tabs
       (BUT This / Left / Right / Unchanged), Save, Save As, Open into ▶
       (Reveal in Finder/Explorer, Open Terminal, Default Viewer), Rename,
       Move to Trash / Recycle Bin, Reload, Read-Only flag, Copy to
       Clipboard ▶ (full path / filename / dir), Move Document ▶ (Start /
       End), Apply Color to Tab ▶ (5 NPP-canonical swatches).
    2. Help ▸ About modal — version, license, repo, OK button.
    3. File ▸ Recent Files (MRU, capped at 10), persisted to
       <config>/Notepad Studio/recent.toml. Stale entries prompt for removal.
    4. macOS dock-icon reopen restores tabs and window placement.
    5. Find dialog "Backward direction" and "In selection" toggles wired
       through to the search engine.
    6. change.log auto-opens on first launch and on every version update.
    7. Windows and Linux: a menu bar inside the window, in Notepad++'s
       order (File, Edit, Search, View, Encoding, Settings, Macro,
       Plugins, ?). Alt or F10 selects it, Alt+<letter> opens a menu,
       arrows, Enter, Esc and access keys work as in Win32 menus, and
       each item shows its shortcut.
    8. Windows: shortcuts that use Shift with a digit or punctuation key
       (Ctrl+Shift+=, Ctrl+Shift+1, Ctrl+Shift+; and others) now fire.
    9. Windows and Linux: picks an installed monospace font instead of
       falling back to a proportional one.
   10. Help ▸ About opens centred in the current window. It used to land
       off-screen, and from a menu or shortcut it opened a second window.
   11. Default shortcuts are now per platform. Windows and Linux use
       Notepad++'s chords; macOS uses the Cmd equivalents or the Mac
       convention. Alt+Shift+Arrow is column selection on Windows/Linux,
       and Ctrl+Shift+L only deletes the line. Saved custom shortcuts
       still load.
   12. Notepad++ editor keys: Ctrl+Backspace / Ctrl+Delete delete a word,
       Ctrl+Shift+Backspace / Ctrl+Shift+Delete delete to the line start
       or end (Option+Delete and Cmd+Delete on macOS), and Ctrl+Up /
       Ctrl+Down scroll one line.
   13. Insert toggles overtype. The status bar shows INS or OVR (click it
       to switch) and the caret becomes an underline while overtyping.
   14. The status bar has Notepad++'s six fields: document type, length
       and lines, Ln / Col with Pos or Sel, line ending, encoding and
       INS / OVR. Messages no longer hide the other fields. Col counts
       tabs to the next tab stop, and length and Pos count a Windows line
       ending as two. Double-click Ln / Col to go to a line.
   15. Crash fixes across editing: typing, Backspace, Delete, IME input,
       column mode, autocomplete and line commands no longer crash next to
       multi-byte characters or after tabs close during a save prompt.
       Backspace and Delete remove a Windows line ending as one unit. A
       misbehaving plugin can no longer take the app down.
   16. Find and Find in Files: in-selection search, result jumps and
       result rows stay on character boundaries, regex replace in files
       keeps Windows line endings, replace-in-files text is literal
       outside regex mode, and very long lines and huge folders no longer
       stall or crash the search.
   17. Markdown preview: deeply nested quotes and lists, long runs of
       closing parentheses, footnotes inside many links and long nested
       emphasis no longer crash or freeze it. Formatting commands work in
       CR and CRLF files and next to multi-byte text.
   18. CSV grid: hostile or ragged CSV files, non-ASCII find and replace,
       and very large fill-series numbers are handled. A CSV that can't be
       written back as UTF-8 is refused instead of blanking the file.
   19. View ▸ Word Wrap and Show Whitespace show a checkmark when on.
   20. Opening a file that is already open switches to its tab instead of
       opening a second copy. Save As onto a file open in another tab is
       refused, as in Notepad++.
   21. Edit commands: line operations, blank operations, case conversion,
       outdent, Begin/End Select and delimiter selection keep line endings
       and non-ASCII text intact and no longer crash next to multi-byte
       characters. Case conversion in column mode converts each row. An
       invalid custom date format is reported instead of doing nothing.
       Text Tools keep line endings and stay responsive on large input.
   22. Windows line endings: Enter, Paste, Insert Blank Line, Duplicate
       Line and Move Line no longer save a Windows file with doubled line
       breaks (CR CR LF). Pasted text takes the document's line ending, and
       Copy and Cut hand other apps the document's own line endings.
   23. Reload uses the same decoding and document parsing as Open. Malformed
       UTF-16 stays read-only, Doc View shows the new file contents, and old
       column selections and selection markers are cleared. A failed reload
       keeps the current buffer and its unsaved edits.
   24. Reload asks before discarding unsaved changes. Cancelling keeps the
       document, and switching or moving tabs while the prompt is open
       cannot make it reload a different file.
   25. Malformed RTF control parameters and excessive group nesting are
       reported as errors instead of crashing the editor.
   26. Doc View handles stale edit positions without overflowing or walking
       past the document. Malformed HTML color values pasted into Doc View
       no longer crash it, and oversized RGB components stay within range.
   27. Rich-document saves protect the original file if writing fails.
       Edited documents with tables or images report a save error instead
       of silently removing that content. Unchanged files still save intact.
   28. Autosave includes every editor window. Saving or closing one window
       no longer removes another window's session entries, unsaved drafts
       or undo backups. Moving a tab keeps a single recovery entry.
   29. ODT files with invalid or oversized space counts return an error
       instead of crashing or allocating unlimited text.
   30. DOCX parsing uses a bounded stack instead of depending on the caller's
       stack size. DOCX and ODT archives with excessive XML nesting, corrupt
       XML, oversized entries or false ZIP sizes are refused before parsing.
   31. Copy in Doc View handles selections through multi-byte characters and
       stale document positions. Extreme DOCX image sizes and hyperlink IDs
       no longer overflow or crash the editor.

   32. Opening a new blank window after the last window closes keeps the
       closed windows' unsaved drafts available for session recovery.

   33. Reopening a closed rich document keeps its native format and Doc View.
       Unrecoverable container backups reopen as unsaved text instead of
       risking the original file. RTF raw edits, dirty/read-only flags and
       CSV grid view survive reopening.

   34. Startup recovery keeps unsaved RTF source edits and safely separates
       unmatched rich-document backups from their original files. Invalid
       native documents report an error instead of reopening as plain text.

   35. Autosave serializes and writes recovery files in the background. Older
       jobs cannot replace newer snapshots, and failed writes preserve the
       previous manifest. Closing flushes the final snapshot; a failed
       recovery write reports an error and prevents a normal window close.

   36. Doc View text and formatting edits survive recovery for RTF, DOCX and
       ODT, even without the original file. Damaged recovery keeps readable
       raw source in a separate unsaved tab. Recovered native documents
       keep their format in Save As. RTF Unicode text now reopens intact.

   37. Language menu — choose any installed syntax mode or None (Normal
       Text), with per-tab checkmarks and rebindable commands. Switching
       modes highlights in the background and preserves file bytes. C++,
       TypeScript/TSX and Markdown now apply their missing highlight rules.

   38. Undo and Redo respect Read-Only in every document view. Rich-document
       history changes mark the document unsaved and schedule recovery.
       Toolbar and context-menu history controls follow the focused view.

   39. Legacy Word documents reject incomplete text ranges and corrupt FAT
       chains with an error instead of opening a truncated or repeated body.

   40. Legacy Word blank paragraphs survive opening and DOCX conversion.
       Compressed characters now follow Word's documented Unicode mapping.

   41. Doc View typing, deletion, paste, formatting and replacements schedule
       recovery. Read-Only blocks these edits, and Replace All schedules a
       single recovery snapshot for the batch.

   42. Save refuses unsupported native raw-source edits instead of silently
       discarding them. Failed Save/Save As keeps the original file and
       unsaved source, including recovered drafts and legacy conversion.

   43. Auto-completion follows the current caret and stays clear of its
       active line. The compact list fits above or below within the pane,
       scales with zoom, supports mouse-wheel selection, and dismisses
       without swallowing outside clicks or leaving invisible suggestions.

   44. Doc View paste and Replace/Replace All undo as one action, restoring
       the complete text, formatting and selection. Empty or blocked edits
       preserve Redo, including nested batches and long history.

   45. Word documents retain styled soft line breaks and tabs, including
       legacy DOC conversion to DOCX. Invalid XML characters in source or
       edited text/links report an error before changing a file.

   46. HTML paste preserves visible Unicode text and quoted links while
       keeping script/style bodies hidden, even with malformed end tags.
       Oversized or excessively nested HTML falls back to plain-text paste.

   47. Auto-completion refilters while typing or deleting, dismisses on
       caret, selection, tab and focus changes, and ignores marked IME text.
       Stale clicks cannot replace another prefix or a read-only document.
       Word scans stay bounded near the caret, including huge identifiers.

   48. HTML paste keeps formatting and links within their matching tags,
       including malformed closing tags. Soft line breaks retain heading,
       list and inline styles, and preformatted text keeps its spaces.

   49. Auto-completion finds broader matches after Backspace and discovers
       words beyond the initial list limit as a prefix narrows. Manual Word,
       Function and combined modes keep their selected candidate source.

   50. Recorded auto-completion replaces its typed prefix during macro
       replay, with the same single Undo and caret as accepting the list.
       Mismatched prefixes and invalid editor contexts are skipped safely.

   51. Undo and Redo recognize the last successful Save across text and
       Doc View edits, grouped actions and branching history. Encoding and
       line-ending changes stay unsaved until restored or written. Invalid
       undo history is refused before it can change unrelated text.

    52. Run menu and F5 open a native command dialog with Browse, document
        variables and recent commands. Arguments are passed literally; launch
        failures keep the command available. Save named commands to the Run
        menu and update, rename or delete them without changing editor Undo.
        Saved-command keyboard shortcuts remain unassigned.

    53. Recorded completion cannot change hidden preview source or an
        inactive window. It requires an active Source/Raw editor context.

    54. Doc View typing preserves emoji and all Unicode characters while
        removing real control codes. Replacing selected text and pressing Enter
        each undo in one step, restoring text, formatting and the selection.

    55. CSV grid Undo/Redo restores saved states and exact source spelling,
        including optional quotes. Metadata and edits in other tabs retain grid
        history; stale grid edits cannot overwrite newer source text. Unchanged
        preview frames avoid copying the entire source file.

    56. Typing Tab over a Doc View selection replaces it in one Undo step,
        restoring text, formatting and the selection when undone.

    57. Completion belongs to its visible editor session: old painted rows
        and wheel events cannot change a reopened list. Switching previews,
        losing focus or deactivating the window dismisses it immediately.
        Source/Raw actions restore editor focus from Doc View and CSV grids.

    58. CSV Save, Save All, tab changes and close commit unfinished cell text.
        Refused commits keep the draft editable.

    59. View > Tab now includes numbered, first/last and next/previous tabs,
        tab reordering and colors. All commands are rebindable; moving tabs
        preserves the selected document and its edits. Open Folder as
        Workspace is also available in the Shortcut Mapper.

    60. Inline text fields handle keyboard editing, Enter and Escape reliably.
        Go to Line returns focus after jumping and explains invalid input.
        Multiline field contents no longer crash display shaping.

   61. Macro playback uses the visible editable source. It leaves preview,
       CSV cells, dialogs, read-only and inactive windows alone, dismisses
       completion before replay, and reports why a replay stops without
       hiding the error behind a success message.

   62. Long input fields shape a bounded window around the caret and clip
       drawing to the field. Full text, selection, clipboard and IME input
       stay intact; repeated full-value cloning and shaping are removed.

   63. Editing macro playback, including repeated runs and nested commands,
       undoes in one step and restores the starting caret and saved state.
       A stopped replay keeps earlier edits in one Undo operation. New
       recordings omit Undo/Redo; older saved history events still replay.

   64. Autocomplete brief mode shrinks the rows as you type; ordinary mode
       retains its rows and follows the current prefix. Selection survives
       filtering, and a retained nonmatching row cannot overwrite newer text.

   65. File menu: Reload from Disk, Open Containing Folder, Default Viewer
       and Close Multiple commands now use the current document and retain
       refused CSV drafts. Reload keeps grid focus. On macOS, CSV Fill Right
       uses Cmd+Alt+R, leaving Cmd+R for Reload. Windows viewer paths go through
       the native shell, preserving literal spaces and special characters.

   66. Unfinished CSV cells recover separately from their verified source.
       Interrupted writes, damaged metadata, stale or read-only source and
       invalid cells retain the full editable draft with a recovery message.
       Grid Save/Enter/Tab keep keyboard focus; very large values render in a
       bounded viewport while copy, edit and recovery retain all text.

   67. Closing the last document, Close All and Close Unchanged no longer
       render an empty tab strip and crash. The replacement blank tab exists
       before the next frame. Close All keeps documents opened after its
       original batch started.

  68. CSV grid focus follows the visible document when switching, closing or
      restoring tabs, including Save As to a text file. Newer dialog fields
      keep their focus, and read-only drafts still block unsafe navigation.

  69. Reload preserves recovered CSV drafts and cancels if the document changes
      during confirmation. Tab-menu commands follow their original document
      after reordering and ignore clicks from closed or replaced menus.

  70. Close confirmations preserve text, document changes and CSV drafts
      created while the prompt was open. Save refuses unopened recovery drafts
      without losing them; opening the CSV grid still exposes their text.

  71. Typing into a focused CSV grid opens the cell editor correctly. The
      first body or header character reaches recovery autosave; later Unicode
      and IME input stays in the cell without editing hidden source text.

  72. Function and combined completion load optional language API definitions
      in the background. Missing files use original keywords; malformed files
      keep the last valid definitions. Manual Function lists select the typed
      prefix and allow choosing another visible member before editing.

  73. Exit checks session recovery before quitting every editor window. A
      failed write keeps documents and CSV drafts open and shows the error
      in the status bar; retrying Exit or window close remains available.

  74. Tab-menu submenus and Rename keep their original document through
      tab moves and late input. Old popups cannot act on newer ones, and
      Rename refuses existing files instead of overwriting them.

  75. Source and CSV IME composition replaces marked text, honors relative
      UTF-16 selection and commits Source input as one Undo action. Changing
      tabs, views or focus retires the old composition range safely.

  76. Edit menu line indentation preserves selected text and uses language
      tab settings. Duplicate Current Line duplicates the caret line, with
      separate rebindable actions and macro events. Unicode, line endings
      and undo are preserved. Indentation currently accepts up to 4096 lines;
      larger selections are refused before editing to keep the UI responsive.

   77. Portable GPUI geometry builds cleanly with newer Rust compilers while
       preserving the exact pixel and font-relative sizing.

   78. Function Parameters Hint shows API signatures and the active argument.
       Alt+Up/Down or popup arrows cycle overloads without editing. Hints
       avoid the caret line and completion list, scan fresh text in the
       background, and dismiss when the editing context changes.

   79. Closing a function call dismisses a successfully opened manual hint
       quietly. An initial request with no open call still explains why.

   80. Exit writes captured recovery in the background. Edits, window changes
       or write failures keep the app open for retry; incomplete loads refuse
       Exit. Final shutdown fences queued recovery writes before releasing
       the instance lock and retains the OS shutdown fallback.

   81. Final Source IME commits and unmarking can open completion at the
       configured character threshold. Preedit, cancelled input and selected
       replacements keep lists closed; Unicode, encoding and undo are preserved.

   82. Shortcut Mapper now exposes all fixed commands in the installed menus,
       including Word Wrap, read-only, encoding and line endings. Existing
       bindings remain compatible; new rows have no default shortcut.

   83. Typing and undo safely update malformed bookmarks, marks and Begin/End
       selection markers. Invalid offsets cannot overflow or leave highlights
       inside a Unicode character; valid annotation positions keep their behavior.

   84. Smart highlighting clears above its 1 MiB limit before copying the
       current line, so a long line or cached word cannot bypass the limit.

   85. Retained Source input callbacks keep their document, caret and focus
       identity, leaving newer tabs, fields and selections untouched. Current
       typing and composition still work; saving a clean CSV grid as text
       restores Source input, including IME.

   86. Delayed completion and parameter hints cannot reopen after editor
       focus leaves and returns, even within one frame. Fresh manual hints
       still work; refocusing the editor or repainting keeps current requests.

    87. Regex Replace and Replace All now expand numbered/named captures,
        optional groups and zero-length matches from fresh matches. Source
        planning runs in the background; stale work and confirmations cannot
        edit a tab after switching away and back. Each document has one Undo
        group. Bounded Source/Doc View limits and unsupported-template errors
        preserve text and history. Replace in Files shares the template parser.

    88. Replace in Files now reports the first unreadable file and the actual
        read error, including files removed or locked after discovery. Failed
        reads leave file contents unchanged.

    89. A retired manual API-completion request now clears its own Loading
        message when the reply arrives. Newer messages, newer requests and
        unrelated reload status remain intact; stale replies cannot reopen
        the completion list.

    90. Completion rows now expire immediately when focus leaves and returns,
        before the next frame. Old painted rows cannot edit text or dismiss a
        fresh list; normal focus, repainting and current clicks still work.

    91. Linux X11 input no longer crashes on malformed input-method text.
        Invalid Reset, Commit and Preedit replies now return protocol errors,
        including Chinese/Korean bytes that previously overflowed or wrapped.

    92. Large line indentation and its Undo/Redo now prepare off the UI
        thread and publish one complete edit only if the editor is unchanged.
        Escape cancels; marks, bookmarks, encoding and saved history survive.
        Costly annotation work also uses the background path. Large macro
        batches and block selections still report their current limits.

    93. Japanese X11 input now consumes ASCII reset markers correctly.
        Invalid or unsupported character designations report an error instead
        of inserting escape markers or silently changing high-byte text.

    94. Indentation also prepares in the background for a few very long
        lines. Unicode row separators cannot hide expensive line scans, and
        a short selection skips scanning the next unselected line.

    95. The X11 input parser no longer panics on nonempty hot-key lists.
        It checks array bounds before allocation, preserves key/state order
        and reserves the correct size when serializing the list.

    96. X11 input replies containing both text and a key symbol now insert
        their text. Synchronous character and key-symbol replies receive the
        required acknowledgement; asynchronous replies remain asynchronous.

    97. Closing an editor now cancels pending replacements and confirmations.
        Late workers cannot change the closed window's Source or rich documents;
        failed recovery saves keep the window and replacement usable.

    98. Old Source input callbacks now retire after real caret or selection
        movement, including same-frame round trips. No-op movement keeps current
        input usable; final selections after edits no longer retire fresh input.

    99. Syntax highlighting after edits runs in bounded background workers,
        coalesces work per tab and cancels stale parses. Undo/Redo, tab close
        and window close cannot publish older highlighting over newer state.

    100. Unsupported Linux XIM transport versions now fall back safely instead
         of crashing. Standard ClientMessage/property delivery stays bounded.

    101. Streamed files finish syntax highlighting in background workers, even
         after switching tabs. Closed windows retire late loading callbacks.

    102. Avoid copying the whole document when syntax highlighting is disabled
         by the configured large-file threshold or the hard size ceiling.

    103. Retire delayed regex replacement results after caret movement, including
         moving away and back; preserve fresh requests and no-op movement.

    104. Refuse saved macros containing unsupported search, replace or save
         events before editing, instead of silently skipping those operations.

    105. Support negotiated Linux XIM multi-message client transport 0.1 with
         bounded reassembly and error handling; mixed transport 0.2 remains open.

    106. Replace All in All Opened Documents now skips read-only documents in
         Normal and Extended modes, so reported replacement totals match the
         writable documents processed.

    107. Find All in Current Document now respects a captured text selection
         and keeps document context for regex anchors and word boundaries.

    108. Linux input-method property messages now validate bounded frames and
         retain unread appended data safely, including long-running sessions.

    109. Reload now reads and parses in background while keeping the current
         document editable. Cancellation or intervening edits keep your text.

    110. Linux input methods can negotiate mixed client-message/property
         transport, with bounded framing and ordered callback delivery.

    111. In-selection Count and Find Next preserve document regex context,
         avoiding false line-start and word-boundary matches.

    112. Recent-file updates persist in background so opening and saving
         do not wait for recent-list writes. Quitting preserves the latest list.

    113. Linux input callbacks keep their order and accepted text is delivered
         before a later protocol error, within bounded memory limits.

    114. Replace in Files refuses oversized literal expansions before writing,
         preserves the original file and reports its path.

    115. Opening, reopening and renaming files schedules syntax highlighting
         in background and rejects results from an older tab or edit.

    116. Searches in rich-document Raw View use the raw selection correctly.
         Switching views clears captured search scopes and prevents raw results
         from selecting hidden source text in Doc View.

    117. Find and Replace in Files supports UTF-16 and ANSI alongside UTF-8,
         preserving encodings, BOMs and line endings. Unsafe conversions report
         the affected file and leave its bytes untouched.

    118. Windows IME input safely handles failed or changing composition reads,
         malformed UTF-16 byte lengths and invalid cursor positions.

    119. macOS text input guards optional output storage and overflowing native
         text ranges when handling substring requests.

    120. Search results follow their original document after tabs move or close.
         Results from changed or reopened documents ask you to search again,
         and old painted rows cannot select results from a newer search.

    121. Restored-session syntax highlighting starts in background after the
         window mounts, prioritizes the active document and refills a bounded
         queue without changing recovered text or undo history.

    122. Clicking a search result preserves its destination selection when
         a pending CSV cell edit prevents switching documents. Committing the
         cell edit lets you retry the result normally.
    123. Replace in Files/Projects refuses stale confirmations after the
         originating window closes or the captured search scope changes.
         Closing the originating Find dialog/editor cancels upcoming file
         work; completed writes remain counted. Directory requests cannot
         overlap, and project search results share a 1,000-row limit.
    124. Replace in Projects counts failed roots even when another root
         replaces files successfully. Its report keeps completed writes,
         the total failure count and the first failed path.
    125. Find All in current/open documents preserves the result panel
         while a directory search or replacement owns it. Finish or cancel
         that operation before starting another search.

    126. Find All in the current Doc View searches current formatted edits
         in background and selects visible text from its result rows.
         Changed or closed documents reject stale results. Closing the
         Find dialog preserves an ongoing document search.

    127. Replace in Files/Projects visits each stable file once, including
         overlapping roots, hard links and Windows case aliases. Explicit
         hidden or normally excluded roots remain eligible for replacement.
         File-identity limits stop further work and report completed writes.

    128. Current-document Doc View search clears its busy state and both
         search messages when the document or query changes while waiting
         for an earlier worker to finish.

    129. Autocomplete arrows stop at the first and last suggestion. Home,
         End and Page Up/Down navigate suggestions without moving the
         editor caret; Shift-selection retains normal editor behavior.

    130. Manual completion waits until IME preedit is committed or unmarked.
         Word, Function and Combined completion preserve marked text and
         its Undo history; loaded API definitions remain available afterward.

    131. Find All in the current Doc View can feed several bounded capture
         chunks per turn. It yields when the worker queue is full, retains
         cancellation, and limits aggregate text and document-node work.

    132. Session recovery keeps duplicate document IDs independent, restores
         closed-tab history and preserves the selected surviving document.
         Failed moves to a new window return unsaved text to its source;
         failed window creation reports an error and retains recovery data.

    133. Plain-text word completion matches the typed case when no API
         definitions are loaded. Loaded API case rules remain authoritative;
         typing, Backspace and accepting a suggestion use the same policy.

    134. Rich-document search highlighting locates intersecting paragraphs
         without scanning every paragraph for each match. Cross-paragraph
         matches and the current-match order stay unchanged. Empty results
         skip paragraph-map validation.

    135. Session recovery reads and decodes documents, imports rich files
         and restores history in background before opening the editor.
         Cancellation and failed recovery retain the original backups.


    136. Rich-document search highlights reuse the displayed text layout
         and resolve positions in bounded chunks. Match order, colors,
         paragraph prefixes and clipping stay consistent.

    137. Preferences ignores nonfinite saved sidebar widths and uses
         the default width so invalid values cannot reach the layout.

    138. Replace All in All Opened searches and edits visible rich-document
         text in Doc View. Native Save and Undo now follow those edits;
         Raw View still replaces source text.

    139. Recorded autocomplete choices replay correctly for API punctuation
         prefixes and manually selected function names, even without the
         original API file. Changed text or editor context still refuses.

    140. Rich-document replacement refuses matches spanning tables or images
         before changing the document. Valid open siblings still replace;
         status reports skipped documents and actual replacement counts.

    141. Completion lists dismiss when a narrow pane cannot fit readable
         labels. Padding, borders and the current font all count toward
         the required width, including at different zoom levels.

    142. Session, settings and theme saves use their own temporary files.
         Existing temporary files and linked documents stay intact;
         failed writes clean up only the temporary they created.

    143. Recovering partly readable settings preserves documents linked to
         an existing recovery copy, while keeping the original settings bytes.

    144. Verbose save backups keep separate versions when saves share a
         timestamp. Existing backup files stay intact; failed copies clean
         up only their own incomplete backup.

    145. Trim Leading Space applies to the selected physical lines. A
         selection ending at the next line's start leaves that line alone;
         rectangular selections are refused and Undo restores the selection.

    146. Local English spelling and grammar checking adds wavy underlines
         and right-click corrections in Normal Text and Markdown Source View.
         Links, email, paths, identifiers and Markdown code are excluded.
         Corrections support Undo, macros and session ignore words. Edit >
         Spelling and Grammar controls each check; see docs/proofreading.md
         for paragraph, document-size and diagnostic limits.
    147. Trim Leading and Trailing Space now changes only the selected
         physical lines, including partially selected lines. Rectangular
         selections are refused, and Undo restores the original selection.

  v0.1.0 — syntax & language:

    1. Tree-sitter syntax highlighting for 25 modes: Rust, Python, C,
       C#, JavaScript, TypeScript, TSX, JSON, Markdown, Bash/Sh/Zsh/Ksh,
       PowerShell, Go, Java, Swift, Ruby, PHP, C++, Lua, HTML, CSS, SCSS,
       YAML, TOML, XML, SQL. Filename detection and shebang fallback.
    2. Bracket matching — depth-aware, skips brackets inside strings and
       comments via tree-sitter span filter.
    3. Auto-indent on Enter — inherits leading whitespace; extra indent
       after `{`, `[`, `(`.
    4. Comment toggle (syntax-aware) — per-language tokens for all 24
       grammars. HTML/XML/Markdown wrap line ranges in <!-- -->.
    5. Settings ▸ Shortcut Mapper — rebind any action with conflict
       detection. Persisted to <config>/Notepad Studio/shortcuts.toml.

  Polish:

    1. Multi-click selection — double-click selects a word, triple-click
       selects a line, quad-click and beyond select all.
    2. Drop-to-open — drag a file onto the window to open it as a new tab.
       Folders are rejected with a status message.
    3. Notepad++-style icon toolbar above the tab strip — File / Edit /
       Search / View / Macro / Plugins groups, monochrome SVGs tinted from
       the active theme. Hover, pressed, and disabled states.

  Beyond:

    1. Column-mode selection + multi-edit — Alt-drag a rectangle. Virtual
       columns extend past end-of-line. Typing, Backspace, Delete, Cut,
       Copy, Paste, and the Cmd/Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Arrow extenders all operate
       rectangularly in a single undo step. Notepad Studio→Notepad Studio block paste
       lands as column paste via a sentinel-tagged clipboard.
    2. Macro record / replay — Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R toggle recording,
       Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+P playback, Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+M opens an inline
       "Run N times" prompt. Save Currently Recorded prompts for a name and
       persists to <config>/Notepad Studio/macros.toml.
    3. Plugin host — native cdylib plugins drop into
       <config>/Notepad Studio/plugins/ and load at startup via libloading. The
       notepad-studio-plugin crate defines the C ABI and ships a safe Plugin
       trait + declare_plugin! macro for authors.
    4. Large-file streaming reads — files larger than 10 MB stream in
       1 MB chunks on the bg executor with status-bar progress and Cancel.
       Files larger than 2 GB show a confirm-open dialog. Syntax
       highlighting is skipped above 50 MB.
    5. Session restore — open tabs, scroll positions, selections, and
       per-window placement persist across restarts. Saved on autosave
       tick, on window close, and on app quit.

  Reliability:

    1. A file you open and save without editing comes back byte for
       byte. LF files are no longer rewritten as CRLF on Windows, mixed
       line endings stay mixed, and UTF-8 BOM, UTF-16 and Latin-1 files
       keep their exact bytes, files over 10 MB included.
    2. Save writes over the file in place and keeps a safety copy until
       the write succeeds. A full disk or a lost drive no longer leaves
       the file empty or cut short.
    3. A damaged settings, session, window, recent-files, macro, shortcut
       or undo file no longer stops startup or wipes everything in it.
       Only the bad entries reset, and the file is kept beside the new
       one as <name>.corrupt. These files and themes are now written
       atomically.
    4. Session restore no longer drops UTF-16 and Latin-1 tabs, and an
       autosave missing from the session list comes back as an untitled
       tab instead of being deleted.
    5. Save As and the save prompt on close save the tab that asked, even
       if you switch or close tabs while the file dialog is open.
    6. Fixed crashes: a backslash before an accented letter in an
       Extended-mode search, input-method typing over a selection that
       ends inside a multi-byte character, a shortcut override gpui
       can't parse, and a hand-edited tab width far out of range (now
       held to 1-16, the Preferences range).
    7. Faster on big files: input-method caret lookups on a 63 MB file
       went from 148 ms to under a microsecond, and word wrap on a
       200 KB line from 3.1 s to 3 ms.


v0.0.7 — Edit ops (Notepad++ parity)

    1. Duplicate Line / Selection — Cmd/Ctrl+D.
    2. Move Line Up / Down — Ctrl+Shift+Up/Down on Linux/Windows,
       Alt+Shift+Up/Down on macOS.
    3. Delete Line — Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+L.
    4. UPPERCASE — Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+U.
    5. lowercase — Cmd/Ctrl+U.
    6. Toggle Block Comment — Cmd+/ on macOS, Ctrl+Q on Linux/Windows.
    7. Toggle Stream Comment — Cmd+Shift+/ on macOS, Ctrl+Shift+Q on
       Linux/Windows.
    8. Insert Blank Line Above / Below — Cmd/Ctrl+Alt+Enter and
       Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Enter.
    9. Trim Trailing Whitespace — from the Edit menu.
   10. Replace All as a single undo step.


v0.0.6 — viewing comfort

    1. Scrollable vertical viewport — only visible lines shape and paint.
    2. Vertical scrollbar — drag thumb to scroll, click track to page-step.
    3. Mouse wheel and trackpad scroll (pixel and line-mode deltas).
    4. PageUp / PageDown navigation with Shift- variants for selection.
    5. Cursor auto-scrolls into view on every move.
    6. Drag-select past the viewport edge auto-scrolls.
    7. Async file and message dialogs — fixes a hard crash on dirty-tab
       close where sync rfd dialogs reentered the gpui AppCell borrow.
    8. Word wrap toggle — View ▸ Word Wrap.
    9. Horizontal scrollbar — visible when wrap is off.
   10. Right-click context menu in the editor body — Undo, Redo, Cut,
       Copy, Paste, Delete, Select All, Toggle Bookmark, Go to Line, Find.
   11. Show whitespace — View ▸ Show Whitespace, · for spaces, → for tabs.
   12. Zoom in/out — Cmd/Ctrl +/-/0, scales chrome, gutter, and editor.
       Persisted to <config>/Notepad Studio/window.toml.
   13. Full-screen toggle — F11.


v0.0.5 — Notepad++ basics

    1. Find bar with incremental search — Cmd/Ctrl+F.
    2. Find Next / Find Previous — F3 / Shift+F3, also Enter / Shift+Enter
       in the find input.
    3. Match-case, whole-word, and regex toggles powered by
       regex::RegexBuilder.
    4. Match counter chip — "X of Y" / "No matches" / "Invalid regex".
    5. All-match highlighting in the editor; current match gets a stronger
       tint.
    6. Replace bar with Replace / Replace All — Ctrl+H, or Cmd+Opt+F on
       macOS.
    7. Go to Line — Ctrl+G, or Cmd+L on macOS.
    8. Find in Files — Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+F. walkdir-backed traversal, async
       off-main search, scrollable results panel, click-to-jump.
    9. "Extended" search mode — \n \r \t \0 \b \\ \xHH \dDDD \oOOO escape
       interpretation in non-regex find/replace.
   10. Mark / Bookmark — F2 toggle, Ctrl+F2 next, Shift+F2 previous,
       Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+F2 clear all. Gutter glyph plus buffer-local set.
   11. Line endings toggle — CRLF / LF / CR. Click the EOL chip in the
       status bar to cycle.
   12. Encoding toggle — UTF-8 / UTF-8 BOM / UTF-16 LE/BE / Latin-1. Click
       the encoding chip to cycle.


v0.0.4 — file I/O & tabs

    1. Multi-tab buffers — switchable buffers in one window with a top tab
       strip.
    2. Cmd/Ctrl+N opens a new empty tab. Cmd/Ctrl+W closes the active tab.
    3. Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab cycle tabs in MRU order.
    4. Ctrl+PageDown and Ctrl+PageUp cycle tabs in visual order.
    5. Cmd/Ctrl+1..9 jump directly to tab N.
    6. Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+W closes every tab; one fresh blank tab is left
       behind.
    7. Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+T restores the most recently closed tab — last 20.
    8. Closing the last tab in a window closes the window.
    9. File ▸ Open via rfd — loads into the active empty tab or a new tab.
       Detects LF / CRLF / CR.
   10. File ▸ Save — Cmd/Ctrl+S. Save As — Cmd+Shift+S on macOS,
       Ctrl+Alt+S on Linux/Windows.
   11. File ▸ Save All — Cmd+Opt+S on macOS, Ctrl+Shift+S on Linux/Windows.
   12. Dirty-prompt on close-tab and Close All — Save / Don't Save /
       Cancel.
   13. Window title and tab strip sync to active tab's file path and
       dirty bit.


v0.0.3 — editable buffer & cross-platform polish

    1. EntityInputHandler — OS / IME text routes into the rope buffer.
    2. Backspace, Delete, Enter, Tab (4 spaces, configurable).
    3. Arrow keys, Home/End, Cmd+Home/End, Cmd+Up/Down for doc bounds.
    4. Alt+Left/Right (macOS) and Ctrl+Left/Right (NPP) for word
       boundaries.
    5. Shift-extend variants on every movement.
    6. Sticky desired column for vertical movement.
    7. Caret blinks every 500 ms via gpui::Timer; resets on activity.
    8. Cut / Paste wired to system clipboard.
    9. Linear undo / redo stacks — Cmd+Z, Cmd+Shift+Z, Ctrl+Y.
   10. Dirty bit reflected in the title bar (● prefix).
   11. Mouse drag keeps selecting when the cursor leaves the window.
   12. File ▸ New Window — Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+N — and macOS dock-icon reopen.
   13. Ctrl- variants for every Cmd- chord; Linux/Windows-friendly
       defaults.
   14. QuitMode::Default — macOS stays alive on last window close,
       Linux/Windows exit.
   15. File ▸ Exit menu entry alongside the macOS Quit.
   16. OS-aware line endings — CRLF default on Windows, LF elsewhere.
   17. Panic hook writes timestamped reports to a platform-correct log
       dir.
   18. Window placement persisted to <config>/Notepad Studio/window.toml.


v0.0.2 — selection & themes

    1. Custom EditorElement with shaped lines and selection paint.
    2. Click + drag to select; shift-click to extend selection.
    3. Cmd/Ctrl+A select all, Cmd/Ctrl+C copy via system clipboard.
    4. FocusHandle, key context, IBeam cursor over the editor.
    5. Light + Dark theme with View ▸ Toggle Theme — Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+T.


